<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The launchd twin of mux-desktop-notifier.service.

  A LaunchAGENT, not a daemon, and the difference is the whole point: an agent
  runs in the user's `gui/<uid>` domain, which macOS only provisions on a real
  login, and a menu bar item cannot exist anywhere else. A LaunchDaemon would
  load at boot and have no session to draw in. Verified on a CI runner, where
  `launchctl bootstrap gui/<uid>` loaded this shape and ran it.

  @PATHS@ ARE SUBSTITUTED BY setup.sh, because a plist has no `%h` and no
  environment expansion: launchd takes these strings literally. systemd's unit
  can ship verbatim with `%h` in it; this one cannot, so the installer writes
  it rather than copying it, and `check` compares what it WOULD write against
  what is there.

  KeepAlive RATHER THAN RunAtLoad ALONE, matching Restart=always on the other
  platform: the tray is meant to outlive a crash, and an agent that exits
  without KeepAlive stays gone until the next login.

  THE PATH IS NAMED FOR THE SAME REASON THE UNIT NAMES IT. An agent inherits
  no login shell, so `mux`, which the daemon re-execs on every poll, would not
  be found. /opt/homebrew/bin comes first because that is where Apple silicon
  Homebrew puts things and a mux installed by `brew` lives there.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>dev.mux.desktop-notifier</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>@EXEC@</string>
  </array>

  <!--
    @PATH@ IS COMPOSED BY setup.sh RATHER THAN SPELLED HERE, because XML
    whitespace inside a <string> is SIGNIFICANT: a plist has no line
    continuation, so the full PATH cannot be wrapped and would be the one
    line in this package over 80 columns. The installer already knows
    BIN_DIR, so it is the right owner anyway, and it can wrap.
  -->
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>@PATH@</string>
  </dict>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    LSUIElement's launchd equivalent. Without it a GUI-session agent can get a
    Dock tile, which a tray daemon must not have: `export` also sets the
    activation policy to Accessory in code, and both are wanted, because the
    policy is set only once an item is published and this covers the window
    before that.
  -->
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Interactive</string>

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>@LOG@</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>@LOG@</string>
</dict>
</plist>
